@misc{gate-G450-membudget-peak-is-zero-without-usr-bin-time,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {On this host membudget records every peak as 0 MB because /usr/bin/time is absent, so the history the autobudget sizes leases from is blind and every autosized lease starts at the 64 MB floor; the unimported fold itself took 511 s here against 379 s on the old host.},
  check    = {unwitnessed},
  note     = {kind=finding by=build-oracle. The witness: scripts/membudget peaks unimported-fold prints max_mb 0, max_sec 511.0, suggested 64MB; the stderr capture shows exec /usr/bin/time: No such file. Also a mode-census blind spot: toolmodes --find unimported returns 0 hits for a mode module_importers dispatches. Rulings: W3 keeps the fold on the roster and sizes the lease for time; W24 files the emerge. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): W3 step 1 (tick 68): module_importers --unimported measured under a 1 GiB membudget lease on this host: 511 s wall (old host 379 s); membudget peaks records max_mb 0 for the label because /usr/bin/time is ABSENT here (sched-batch: exec /usr/bin/time: No such file or directory), so the peak column that AGDA_MB=auto sizes leases from is blind on this host - every autosized lease lands on the 64 MB floor and climbs the OOM ladder, and buildtime --mem reads nothing new. Also: toolmodes --find unimported reports 0 hits while the mode dispatches (module_importers is in substrate/, outside the mode census's 106-tool population). Operator rulings (tick 68): keep the fold on the roster and size the per-label lease for TIME as well as memory (W3 continues as agent work); file the missing time binary as W24, operator emerges sys-process/time.}
}

@misc{gate-G451-suite-run-had-no-deadline-now-sized-from-the-label-ledger,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {suite_run ran every suite with no timeout; it now budgets a per-label deadline from the same ledger the memory lease reads - twice the slowest recorded run, rounded up to a minute, clamped loudly - and reports an overrun as its own kind with no stamp written.},
  check    = {selftest:substrate/suite_run_selftest.py,--selftest},
  note     = {kind=finding by=build-oracle. The witness: substrate/suite_run_selftest.py --selftest exits 0 at 15/15 with a 60 s sleep fixture killed at an explicit 1 s deadline and classified timeout; label_lease 21/21. The ledger's second column held every run's wall time since the first lease and nothing had ever read it - the same built-then-unconsumed shape the memory sizer had (gate-G374). ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): W3 (ruled tick 68: keep the fold on the roster, size the lease for TIME too): suite_run.run had NO timeout at all - a hung suite hung the build and an honest 511 s fold was indistinguishable from a hang. label_lease now reads the ledger's wall-seconds column it never read: deadline(ledger, label, default_s) = twice the slowest recorded run rounded up to a whole minute (511 s -> 1080 s), floor 120 s, ceiling 3600 s clamped LOUDLY naming the label and the consequence (may kill an honest run); suite_run.timeout_for(label) applies it with SELFTEST_TIMEOUT_S winning outright as SELFTEST_MB does; a run past its deadline is its own kind, timeout, exit 1, NO stamp (make re-runs it), the partial output re-emitted and the deadline printed beside the slowest run. label_lease 21/21, suite_run 15/15 (a 60 s sleep fixture under a 1 s explicit deadline).}
}

